WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.578
Upper stem sections form later.

00:00:03.039 --> 00:00:06.125
A higher sample can contain a shorter record.

00:00:06.579 --> 00:00:10.039
Distinguish a ring count from age since germination.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.717
A tree takes time to reach the sampling height.

00:00:14.171 --> 00:00:19.584
The ring count at that height alone does not measure the full time since germination.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:22.704
Dormancy and reactivation.

00:00:23.166 --> 00:00:25.573
Cell production and maturation.

00:00:26.034 --> 00:00:28.771
Activity declines toward dormancy.

00:00:29.231 --> 00:00:33.692
Seasonal changes in cell dimensions and wall thickness help make growth increments

00:00:33.705 --> 00:00:35.080
recognizable.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:39.652
Interpret boundaries through those anatomical changes.

00:00:40.455 --> 00:00:41.936
Cell division.

00:00:42.391 --> 00:00:44.030
Cell enlargement.

00:00:44.484 --> 00:00:47.773
Secondary wall formation and lignification.

00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:49.740
Maturation.

00:00:50.194 --> 00:00:53.247
A newly divided cell is not finished wood.

00:00:53.707 --> 00:00:59.360
Enlargement and wall formation occur in stages that overlap through the growing season.

00:01:00.163 --> 00:01:02.282
L: longitudinal.

00:01:02.744 --> 00:01:04.481
R: radial.

00:01:04.942 --> 00:01:06.810
T: tangential.

00:01:07.271 --> 00:01:13.310
Distinguishing longitudinal, radial and tangential directions helps identify the orientation

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:17.028
of a section or image within the original tree.

00:01:17.831 --> 00:01:21.594
Transverse section R–T plane, perpendicular to L.

00:01:21.699 --> 00:01:24.128
Radial section L–R plane.

00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:27.340
Tangential section L–T plane.

00:01:27.794 --> 00:01:31.386
The same tissue looks different in different cutting planes.

00:01:31.846 --> 00:01:36.616
Relate the section name to the three-dimensional arrangement of its cells.

00:01:37.419 --> 00:01:41.275
Tracheid An elongated cell linked to neighbours through pits.

00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:45.908
Vessel A chain of vessel elements connected through perforations.

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:50.277
Vessel elements and tracheids differ in shape and arrangement.

00:01:50.737 --> 00:01:55.556
Examine their context as well as the size of an individual opening.

00:01:56.359 --> 00:01:58.952
Examples: sugi and hinoki.

00:01:59.414 --> 00:02:03.118
Earlywood tends to have larger lumens and thinner walls.

00:02:03.572 --> 00:02:07.236
Latewood tends to have smaller lumens and thicker walls.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:12.828
In the softwood schematic, follow changes in tracheid lumen size and walls.

00:02:13.282 --> 00:02:18.672
Real specimens also require attention to species and sampling position.

00:02:19.475 --> 00:02:22.608
Examples: chestnut and konara oak.

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:26.958
Large vessels are concentrated near the start of an increment.

00:02:27.411 --> 00:02:31.536
Examine the distribution, not just a single row of pores.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.911
Large earlywood vessels are useful clues in ring-porous wood.

00:02:36.372 --> 00:02:41.716
Confirm them against the anatomical sequence across the whole growth increment.

00:02:42.519 --> 00:02:45.027
Examples: beech and maples.

00:02:45.482 --> 00:02:49.370
Vessel sizes are comparatively similar within an increment.

00:02:49.824 --> 00:02:53.725
Fibre and parenchyma patterns can help locate boundaries.

00:02:54.179 --> 00:02:58.640
Vessel size alone may not reveal a boundary in diffuse-porous wood.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.296
Inspect arrangement and changes in other tissues as well.

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:05.442
Softwood.

00:03:05.891 --> 00:03:07.285
Ring-porous.

00:03:07.733 --> 00:03:09.285
Diffuse-porous.

00:03:09.733 --> 00:03:15.325
The three wood types differ in the anatomical evidence used to locate boundaries, not simply

00:03:15.338 --> 00:03:18.732
in how easy or difficult their rings appear.
